WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.634
Actors are now reinforcing

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.802
the writers on the picket line

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:03.603
in Hollywood.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:04.838
They're on strike to demand,

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:05.338
in part

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.007
better compensation from the studios

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.441
and the streaming giants.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.010
The struggle for fair pay

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:10.810
being highlighted

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.612
by the stars of one of the first

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:15.482
hit shows from the streaming era

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:18.752
My God, I'm about to be so rich.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.454
And you

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:26.059
to watch

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.030
That was Kimiko Glenn,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.499
who played Brooke So-so on Netflix's

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.167
Orange is the New Black.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:34.801
She earned

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:38.271
just $27 on that residual track

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.406
you saw there.

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:40.206
Her frustration

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.475
being echoed by many of her costars.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:44.544
Leah Delaria telling The New Yorker,

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:45.845
I get $20.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.914
I would love to know how much money did

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.783
Ted Sarandos make last year.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:50.950
Well, here's the answer.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.219
Sarandos, who is the CEO of Netflix,

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.487
according to the company's

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.389
financial statements, last year,

00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:00.093
he received $20 million in base salary,

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:01.461
more than 50 million,

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:02.762
if you include the stock

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.798
and the options and bonuses.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:05.532
Emma Myles,

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.866
Another Orange is the New Black

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.501
Alum, told The New Yorker.

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.170
When you're a kid, you have this idea.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.171
Once I'm on something

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:13.339
that people actually see, I'll be rich

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.174
and you'll look around

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:15.141
after being on that

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:16.109
hit show and you're like,

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.644
Wow, I'm still in the same one

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:18.912
bedroom apartment.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.414
Was this how it's supposed to be now?

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:24.751
Emma Miles and Leah Delaria join me now.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:25.685
Emma

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:27.520
Can you

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.756
explain to the audience,

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:30.523
first of all, what

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.492
what are residual checks? Right.

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.594
What are they supposed to be?

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.931
And what does that financially look like

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.531
for you

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:39.632
once you've been on a show

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.235
like Orange is the New Black?

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:43.303
Okay.

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.537
Well, in terms

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.907
of how our business works, residuals

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:52.378
typically are paid out for network cable

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:54.647
movies,

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.216
and they're basically

00:01:56.216 --> 00:01:59.786
a share of the profits that come from

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.022
the revenue from being on a network.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.657
So there's a lot of advertising,

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:05.458
there's a lot of money

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.360
that comes with that.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.096
So the residuals for network

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.365
is they're a lot,

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.134
lot higher, like much higher.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:18.872
And we have certain bases in our contract

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.640
that say that they have to be

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.308
at a certain percentage

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.310
that have to be calculated that way.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.247
Now, in terms of residuals, for Orange,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.616
we were not based off

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:30.783
of network and cable.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:31.784
We were off

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:32.819
we were based off of this

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.821
new thing called streaming,

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.390
which basically didn't exist

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.125
when we started the show.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.028
So the contract that we had reflected

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.198
like webisodes and web series

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.966
and things that were on YouTube

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:48.902
and promotional videos and

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.372
the the way that those

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:54.374
residuals are calculated,

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:56.876
we are actually not sure because

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.613
for the millions upon millions of dollars

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.247
in revenue

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.282
that these companies,

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:03.183
these streaming companies

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:05.385
particularly are raking in.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:07.353
There was not really much of a kickback

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.454
for any of the people

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.156
involved on the creative side.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:12.792
So for something like an episode of Law

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.895
and Order SVU that you would have gotten

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:19.999
like a good like several thousand

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.669
dollars from it re-airing on television,

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:25.572
there's a you get about,

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:29.342
I don't know, $20 for people to be able

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.243
to watch Orange

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:32.779
all five seasons of Orange in perpetuity.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:36.549
And and that's a pretty big discrepancy.

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:37.350
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.353
I mean the number of people watching on

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.221
streaming services

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.689
is sort of like a black box.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.591
The streamers don't

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.293
necessarily want transparency about that.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.528
But but Leah,

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:49.229
you said that

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:49.529
there was

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:51.497
this accidental moment of clarity

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.266
from Netflix CEO Ted Sarandos

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.867
because he compared oranges

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:55.468
The New Black,

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:56.903
which was a groundbreaking

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.304
show in so many ways,

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:01.241
to the other hit show, Game of Thrones.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.076
What did that tell you

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.679
Well, we were all at a party

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.348
when when Ted said that

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:10.450
that we were bigger than Game of Thrones.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.485
And we were all it was.

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:12.752
You know,

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:14.354
we were a very tight knit cast

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:15.521
we hung out a lot

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:16.322
and we were all kind of

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.924
hanging out together.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.892
And he made that statement.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:20.760
And it was a complete

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.062
aha moment for everyone at that party.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.764
We all just looked at each other

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.000
And, of course, give me the money.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.370
Where is the money?

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.438
I'm still I'm still living

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.207
in my Bushwick apartment.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.142
Where the money

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.146
I mean, it's a really good question.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:40.580
Look,

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.950
the stars of Orange is the New Black

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.020
were they really did become famous

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:50.023
And as you noted in your comments

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:51.357
to The New Yorker, when you

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:52.325
when you're famous

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:53.726
and people are stopping you on the street

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:54.360
and you go home

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.228
and you pick up that jacket,

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.195
it says, 20 bucks

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:59.766
What does that feel like?

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.200
I mean, it feels like.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.935
Well, you know.

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.605
Yeah, yesterday

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.074
yesterday I'm in my a building here

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.609
and I'm going I'm

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:11.344
walking into the elevator

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:14.180
to go do my laundry.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:16.549
So, you know, my hair is in curlers.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.885
And I'm wearing a bathrobe,

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:20.753
and you know what I mean?

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.622
And there's a couple of guys

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:23.723
in the elevator

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:25.491
that are fixing the elevator,

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:28.294
and they immediately and not in a

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:29.962
in a first language, right?

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.898
They immediately start screaming.

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:32.465
They scream

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:35.201
like teenage girls to elevator repairman

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:37.170
because they wanted to get it.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.406
They wanted to get a picture with me.

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:41.874
So and I love to be

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:43.142
I'm always gracious to my fans.

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:43.976
So as

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.311
you know, without our fans,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:47.280
we'd be nothing

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.382
but it's kind of rough when I my last

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:50.049
residual check

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:52.785
came in June, it was for $20.27.

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:54.654
And like, you know,

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.190
every time I have to stop and

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.224
get my picture taken,

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:01.094
that's 20 to 20 times a day.

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.161
You know,

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.130
it seemed like first world problems,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.898
but that's how famous we are.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.900
And we're making $20.27.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:08.935
Go ahead, Emma.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:09.936
Well, there's also,

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:11.804
there's also like an attitude that comes

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:12.505
this idea

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:15.541
that like we like in terms of the fans

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:16.776
that can be really aggressive

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:17.577
because Lee is right.

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:19.645
The fans are amazing for the most part,

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:20.313
but there are

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:22.448
those fans that are very aggressive

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.218
and they can, they can be like, well,

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:26.119
I pay your salary.

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.354
Like, I watch I watch your show.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.623
So I'm entitled

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:31.924
to whatever time

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:32.492
I want to take

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:34.994
from you or bite or a picture

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:35.962
and I'm just like,

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:37.730
if you're responsible for my salary,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.197
we are

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:40.500
talking to you.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.301
So so

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:42.769
let's I

00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:45.037
want to start with you on this one.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.707
How do you see this strike ending?

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.275
Both sides are really dug in.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:52.779
The streamers are saying and the the

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:54.113
the studios are saying,

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:55.982
we can't afford to pay more.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.550
How does this end?

00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:00.453
I mean, they can afford to pay more.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:02.054
That's that's completely.

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:03.322
They're saying two different things.

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.191
They're telling all of the actors

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:06.192
and all of the writers

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:07.293
and all of the creatives

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:08.828
that they don't have the money.

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:09.796
And meanwhile,

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:11.464
they're telling all of their shareholders

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:12.365
that they're making more money

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:13.266
than they ever have been.

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:14.767
So both things can't be true.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.135
At the same time.

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:17.837
How do I see this ending?

00:07:17.837 --> 00:07:21.908
I mean, I want it to end well.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:23.342
I think that we're

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:24.444
not going to stop fighting

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:25.945
until we get

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.480
certain protections in place

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.782
so that what happened to us on

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:30.383
Orange

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:31.784
can't happen to any

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.886
other cast ever again.

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:37.056
I mean, we should be fairly compensated.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:38.458
They are managing

00:07:38.458 --> 00:07:41.561
billions of dollars off of work

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:44.530
that is being kind of shoved out into

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:45.998
into the universe.

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.934
And and no one

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.936
can support their livelihoods.

00:07:49.936 --> 00:07:51.170
I don't have health insurance.

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:52.839
And of

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:53.573
course, there

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:54.841
are the issues related

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:56.609
to artificial intelligence.

00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:58.845
These are deep, difficult problems.

00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:00.480
But I'm just glad that

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.048
both of you are so famous

00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:03.449
that people stop you on the streets

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:05.852
still and demand photographs.

00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:06.752
I have a miles

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.120
and I get a large

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.722
share, and you're both very bad.

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:12.391
I'd rather have a chance.

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:14.260
I'd rather have a bathtub.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.561
Fair enough. Fair enough.

00:08:15.561 --> 00:08:16.729
Thank you both very much.

